zl1 installer self-test -- the two installers that write device state, and the two defects it found
2026-09-23. Host-side. Device: EDL (05c6:9008, port 3-3). Nothing here touched the device.
================================================================================

1. the defect: a helper function's loop variable is the CALLER's
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ git show HEAD:scripts/install-retire-debug-keeper.sh | sed -n "/RK_EOF/,/^RK$/p" > head-applier.sh
has_address() {
    i=$(iface)
    [ -n "$i" ] || return 1
    live=$(ip -4 addr show dev "$i" 2>/dev/null | awk '/inet /{printf "%s ", $2}')
    case " $live " in
    *" 192.168.2.15/24 "*) return 0 ;;
    *" 10.15.19.82/24 "*) return 0 ;;
    esac
    return 1
}
   ... and the gate it is called from:
# 1. the gate: an address must exist before we take away the thing that used to provide one.
i=0
while [ "$i" -lt "$WAIT_S" ]; do
    has_address && break
    sleep 1
    i=$((i + 1))

   iface() is called by has_address(), has_address() is called from that loop, and a shell
   function's variables are the caller's -- so the counter is left holding the string 'rndis0'.

2. the three cases, run against the HEAD applier in a fake device
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A. neither rndis0 nor usb0 exists  (the loop's own guard is what is being tested)
$ timeout 10 sh head-run.sh          # WAIT_S shortened to 3 so this is quick
   rc=124  <-- still running when the timeout fired: it never advances, it SPINS
           the arithmetic is $((' + 1)) = 1 every time round, so 1 < 45 forever.
           On the device that is a full core, in the script whose whole purpose is to give
           a core back (and systemd's TimeoutStartSec=180 is the only thing that ends it).

   B. rndis0 exists AND carries 192.168.2.15/24
$ timeout 10 sh head-run.sh
   (it gets past the gate: has_address succeeds on the FIRST iteration, so '&& break' fires and
    the fatal arithmetic is never reached. The kill and the verify do work in this case -- which
    is why the harness's kill assertions PASS against HEAD. The defect is not 'it never kills'.)

   C. rndis0 exists, NO address        <-- the case the refusal gate exists for
$ timeout 10 sh head-run.sh
   /tmp/kp/head-run.sh: 68: Illegal number: rndis0
   rc=2
           dash does not treat that as a failed assignment: it prints Illegal number and EXITS
           THE SCRIPT. So the applier dies on its first iteration -- before the refusal check,
           before the log line, before anything. The safety property that matters most,
           'it refuses to kill when it cannot see an address', was never exercised, and an
           operator would see a FAILED UNIT AND NOTHING IN THE LOG.

           (The outcome is still the safe one -- it died before killing anything -- but by
            accident, not by design, and with the explanation missing.)

3. the second defect: a log line that splits into two journal records
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ the applier with two keeper processes, at HEAD:
   logger -t zl1-retire-keeper retiring keeper pids=[900
   logger -t zl1-retire-keeper 905] cpu_ticks_so_far=20 (HZ=100) uptime=... matched= [...]
   keeper_pids echoes one pid per line, PIDS=$(keeper_pids) kept the newlines, and log() builds
   ONE line. The second record starts mid-sentence, so the one line whose job is to say WHICH
   processes are being taken away is the one that cannot be read as a line.

4. the fixes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A shell function's variables are its CALLER's -- there are no locals here. `iface()` used to be
# `for i in rndis0 usb0`, and `has_address()` is called from the wait loop, so the loop's counter was
# left holding the string "rndis0" every time round:
#
#   i=0; while [ "$i" -lt "$WAIT_S" ]; do has_address && break; sleep 1; i=$((i + 1)); done
#
# With rndis0 present -- i.e. always, on this port -- `i=$((i + 1))` is then arithmetic on "rndis0":
# dash prints "Illegal number: rndis0" and **exits the whole script** (status 2), so the applier died
# on its first iteration, before the refusal gate and before the kill, and `--install --now` would have
# reported a failed unit with nothing in the log. With neither interface present it was worse: `iface`
# echoed nothing, `i` became empty, `$(( + 1))` is 1, and the loop never advanced -- a spin at a full
# core, in the one script whose purpose is to give a core back. Found offline by
# scripts/host/zl1-installers-selftest.sh, which is also what keeps it found.
#
# So: every helper's variables are underscore-prefixed, and the counter has a name of its own.
iface() {
    for _i in rndis0 usb0; do
        [ -e "/sys/class/net/$_i" ] && { echo "$_i"; return; }
    done
}
   ...
# keeper_pids echoes one pid per line, so join them: `log` builds ONE line, and an embedded newline
# would split it into two journal records with the second starting mid-sentence. (Found by
# scripts/host/zl1-installers-selftest.sh, with two keeper processes -- which is the case this log line
# exists to make visible.)
PIDS=$(keeper_pids | tr '\n' ' ')
PIDS=${PIDS% }
CMDS=""
for p in $PIDS; do
    t=$(awk '{print $14 + $15}' "/proc/$p/stat" 2>/dev/null) || t=0
    TICKS=$((TICKS + ${t:-0}))
    c=$(tr '\000' ' ' < "/proc/$p/cmdline" 2>/dev/null)

