================================================================================
8. what this round does NOT show
================================================================================
  * NOTHING ABOUT THE DEVICE. Every container property, /proc entry, kernel parameter, unit and
    address in this run is a fixture. The device is in EDL and none of it has been executed there.
  * The two installers have still never run on the device. What is proven is what they WRITE and when
    they refuse -- which is what they were written for, and what could be settled offline.
  * The `systemctl start <unit>` path is simulated by a stub that runs the unit's own ExecStart. That
    is the device's behaviour as systemd documents it (Type=oneshot, RemainAfterExit=yes), not a
    measurement of this device's systemd.
  * The keeper's cost (docs 72's full core, the 5.5/6.1/2.7 C drop) is NOT re-measured here -- that
    measurement is owed on the device, and it is the one that decides whether the retirement is worth
    its risk.
  * install-no-edl-on-panic.sh passes its own section, which says nothing about whether clearing
    download_mode actually keeps the device out of EDL -- its own header says the forced watchdog bite
    still happens and whether the bootloader enters EDL independently of the flag is untested. That can
    only be tested by panicking the device, and it should not be.
